-- The two alternatives of the commencement clause, each kept as a period and the printed date it runs from. The row does not add a period to a date and does not apply the Interpretation Law's counting: nothing below is a date of effect.
`alternative 1 of the commencement clause` MEANS
    `A commencement alternative` WITH
        `what it waits for` IS "three months from the day of publication of the amending law"
        `the number of months` IS 3
        `counted from the date printed on` IS (Date 15 6 2010)
        `where it is printed` IS "SH 2243 printed page 544, section 14; the issue's footer prints 15.6.2010"

`alternative 2 of the commencement clause` MEANS
    `A commencement alternative` WITH
        `what it waits for` IS "the entry into force of the regulations made under the sections the clause names; the regulations whose opening words name them (KT 7445) begin six months from their publication"
        `the number of months` IS 6
        `counted from the date printed on` IS (Date 26 11 2014)
        `where it is printed` IS "KT 7445 printed page 276, regulation 96; the issue's footer prints 26.11.2014"

-- The later of the two is NOT computed: NOTHING. The upper bound is justified only by arithmetic (six months from 26.11.2014 lies well before 2016-01-01) and is a bound, not a commencement date.
